    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">Property adjuster salary in 2026 typically runs from about $45,000 for a trainee to well over $100,000 for a senior large loss specialist. Where you land depends on whether you work a desk or the field, whether you handle homes or commercial buildings, and how good you are at writing an accurate estimate.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Most staff property adjusters earn $55,000 to $90,000 a year. Desk property adjusters commonly make $55,000 to $75,000, field property adjusters $62,000 to $90,000, and senior or large loss property adjusters $85,000 to $120,000+. Field roles often add a company car, gas card and benefits worth $8,000 to $15,000.</div>

<p>Property is the largest specialty in claims. Homeowners, renters, condo and commercial property policies generate a steady stream of water, wind, hail, fire and theft claims every day, and a surge of them after every major storm. That volume is why property adjusting is where many people start, and where many build long careers.</p>

<h2 id="property-adjuster-salary-by-role">Property Adjuster Salary by Role and Level</h2>

<p>The table below shows typical staff (W-2) pay ranges based on industry estimates and the job postings we track. Treat them as ranges, not guarantees: carrier, region and your claim authority all matter.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Typical base salary</th><th>What the job looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Property adjuster trainee</td><td>$45,000 - $60,000</td><td>Classroom and shadowing, small desk claims, learning Xactimate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desk property adjuster</td><td>$55,000 - $75,000</td><td>Phone and photo-based claims, contractor estimates, virtual inspections</td></tr>
<tr><td>Field property adjuster</td><td>$62,000 - $90,000</td><td>Daily on-site inspections, roof climbs, writing estimates in the field</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial property adjuster</td><td>$75,000 - $105,000</td><td>Businesses, apartments, business interruption, higher authority</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior / large loss property adjuster</td><td>$85,000 - $120,000+</td><td>Major fires, total losses, complex coverage, experts and attorneys</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<p>For context, the BLS reports a median of $76,790 a year for claims adjusters, appraisers, examiners and investigators as of May 2024. Property adjusters sit around that median, with field and senior roles above it. Our main <a href="/">insurance adjuster salary</a> guide covers the full picture across every line.</p>

<h2 id="desk-vs-field-property-adjuster">Desk vs Field Property Adjuster Pay</h2>

<p>The single biggest split in property adjuster salary is desk versus field.</p>

<h3>Desk property adjusters</h3>
<p>Desk adjusters (sometimes called inside or virtual adjusters) handle claims by phone, email, video and photos. They review contractor estimates, approve smaller water and wind losses, and manage a high caseload. The work is predictable, usually Monday to Friday, and often remote. Pay is lower, commonly $55,000 to $75,000, because the claims are smaller and the job carries no travel.</p>

<h3>Field property adjusters</h3>
<p>Field adjusters drive to the loss, inspect damage, measure, photograph, climb roofs and write the estimate on site. Base pay commonly runs $62,000 to $90,000. The real gap is wider once you add the perks:</p>
<ul>
<li>Company car or monthly vehicle allowance</li>
<li>Gas card or mileage reimbursement</li>
<li>Laptop, tablet, phone and ladder provided</li>
<li>Full benefits package and retirement match</li>
</ul>
<p>Those extras often add $8,000 to $15,000 of value a year. A field adjuster earning $72,000 with a company car is, in practical terms, ahead of a desk adjuster at $75,000 who drives a personal vehicle and pays for gas.</p>

<h2 id="residential-vs-commercial-property">Residential vs Commercial Property Claims</h2>

<p>Home insurance adjuster salary sits at the lower and middle part of the property range. Residential claims are high volume: a pipe burst under a sink, a hail-damaged roof, a kitchen fire. Most residential adjusters stay in the $55,000 to $85,000 band depending on desk or field.</p>

<p>Commercial property pays more because the files are harder. You deal with:</p>
<ul>
<li>Business interruption and extra expense calculations</li>
<li>Coinsurance and valuation disputes</li>
<li>Multiple buildings, tenants and mortgagees on one loss</li>
<li>Building code upgrades, engineers and forensic accountants</li>
</ul>
<p>Carriers rarely put new adjusters on commercial files. Most commercial property adjusters have several years of residential field experience first, and their pay reflects it.</p>

<h2 id="large-loss-senior-property-adjuster-salary">Large Loss and Senior Property Adjuster Salary</h2>

<p>Senior property adjuster salary is where the role becomes a true six-figure job. Large loss adjusters handle the claims that can run into the hundreds of thousands or millions of dollars: total fire losses, major commercial water damage, collapse. They negotiate with public adjusters and attorneys, hire experts, and hold significant settlement authority.</p>

<p>Typical pay for senior and large loss property adjusters is $85,000 to $120,000+, with some general adjusters (GAs) earning more. Getting there usually takes five to ten years, a clean file-quality record, and designations such as the AIC (Associate in Claims) or a CPCU.</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask for large loss shadowing or a "complex claims" rotation early. Carriers promote people who have already shown they can manage a big file, and that exposure is often worth more than a small raise in your current role.</div>

<h2 id="skills-that-raise-property-adjuster-pay">Skills That Raise Property Adjuster Pay</h2>

<h3>Xactimate</h3>
<p>Xactimate is the estimating software used across most of the property industry. Adjusters who can build a clean, accurate estimate quickly, including sketching rooms, roof diagrams and line-item pricing, are the ones who get field roles, large loss promotions and independent adjuster deployments. Xactimate certification levels are a common line on job postings.</p>

<h3>Roof inspections</h3>
<p>Hail and wind claims are a huge share of property work, and many come down to the roof. You need to climb safely, identify hail versus wear or blistering, measure pitch and squares, and document it. Adjusters comfortable on steep roofs are in demand, especially in hail states like Texas, Colorado and Oklahoma.</p>

<h3>Coverage knowledge</h3>
<p>Knowing the policy language around wear and tear, matching, ordinance and law, and mold limits lets you close files without escalation. That speed and accuracy is exactly what carriers measure, and it shows up in reviews and raises.</p>

<h2 id="field-property-adjuster-salary-staff-vs-independent">Staff vs Independent Property Adjusters</h2>

<p>Everything above describes staff adjusters on salary. Independent adjusters (IAs) handle property claims too, but as 1099 contractors paid per claim on a fee schedule, often roughly $350 to $600 for small claims and $1,000 to $2,000 for claims in the $25,000 to $50,000 range. IAs pay their own expenses and get no benefits, and their income swings with storm activity.</p>

<p>Many IAs work catastrophe deployments after hurricanes and hail storms. If that path interests you, read our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/independent-adjuster-salary/">independent adjuster salary</a> breakdown and our guide to <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/cat-adjuster-salary/">cat adjuster salary</a>.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-become-a-property-adjuster">How to Get Hired as a Property Adjuster</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get licensed.</strong> Most states require an adjuster license. Many non-residents use a Texas all-lines license as a designated home state license because of its broad reciprocity. An online pre-licensing course such as the one at <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a> (code joe20 takes 20% off) covers the exam.</li>
<li><strong>Learn Xactimate basics.</strong> Even a trainee who can sketch a room stands out.</li>
<li><strong>Apply to carrier trainee programs.</strong> Large carriers hire property trainees regularly and train them on the job.</li>
<li><strong>Move to the field.</strong> After a year or two on the desk, field property roles raise both pay and future options.</li>
</ol>

<p>Pay also varies by location. Check <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a> for regional estimates, or plug your own numbers into the <a href="/salary-calculator/">Adjuster Salary Calculator</a>.</p>

<h2 id="is-property-adjusting-worth-it">Is a Property Adjuster Career Worth It?</h2>

<p>For most people, yes. Property claims will not disappear: every storm season produces a wave of them, and complex property losses still need a human on site. The BLS projects overall claims employment to decline 5% from 2024 to 2034 as simple claims are automated, but it still expects thousands of openings each year from retirements and turnover.</p>

<p>The adjusters who earn the most are the ones who move past simple desk claims: field inspections, commercial files, large loss and strong estimating. Build those skills deliberately and a property adjuster salary in the $85,000 to $120,000 range is a realistic mid-career target.</p>
